WEBVTT

NOTE
Automatic English captions. Generated locally and checked with additional audio decodes; not fully audited by listening.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.740
When a workflow runs, the output only
tells you where it ended up. The log tells

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:10.360
you how it got there. It shows which
blocks ran, what each one received, what

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:15.360
it returned, how long it took, where a
value originally came from. That matters

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:19.600
when something looks wrong because
debugging a workflow usually means tracing

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:24.880
one bad result backwards until you find
the step that caused it. Let's take a look

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:30.500
at a real run and read the log. Here's a
workflow processing a support ticket. Four

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:35.180
blocks start to finish. Every time a
workflow runs, Sim records exactly what

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:37.300
happened. That record is called a log.

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:43.360
The log lists every block in the order it
ran, along with its timing, so you can see

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:46.240
exactly where the 12 seconds of this run
were spent.

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:53.800
Selecting a block shows everything it
actually did. This agent made three tool

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:57.500
calls, a customer lookup, and two
knowledge searches, and the log records

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:03.220
each call, along with the tokens it used.
The log also shows where every value came

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:07.320
from. This row was built from the
function's result. The function read the

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:11.760
agent's answer, and the agent read the
customer's message. To debug a workflow,

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:16.540
you read the log backwards from the wrong
value step -by -step to its source.

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:25.480
Every run produces a log automatically.
When a workflow does something unexpected,

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:27.540
the log is the first place to look.
